ervation banking  industry or the carbon investment  industry for the logging industry very  often their interests are not going to  be aligned and we have some work to do  to try and find ways to align those  interests better and that leads me to  Wall Street because I thought my last  lecture here I five and I was intrigued  by this phenomena and went down to visit  liberty park or Zuccotti Park whatever  you want to call it over the weekend and  I was struck by the emphasis there on  the equity issues but no consideration  no discussion that I could see or very  little of the environmental issues and  I'm wondering if they're people here who  have thoughts on what how do these  things relate to each other right is  there some Occupy Wall Street's very  concerned very focused on inequity in  American society in capitalism generally  about the rich not pulling their weight  not paying their fair share of taxes and  I'm wondering if there are any opinions  or views here on what does this have to  do with biodiversity if you were to go  yourself to New York or to the Occupy  Wall Street events here in new haven for  that matter what would you say about  biodiversity and ecosystems what does it  have to do with that there's it's  there's good research for example on the  effect of inequality on health outcomes  in many countries there's pretty good  research on the effect of inequality on  product  growth and in some countries and there  are a few studies that look at the  relation between inequality and and  biodiversity loss but I think they're a  little bit tenuous I'd haven't seen the  particular paper you're referring to but  it's not a it's not a well-researched  area it's not like I mean it would be  nice to be able to write on a t-shirt  inequality destroys the earth but we're  we don't really know that and I think it  maybe that's something that could be of  interest for as a research agenda people  who want to pursue this try and  understand what are the links between  inequality and natural resource use does  it make it easier or harder to to find  solutions or does it exacerbate the  problem in some way and can we measure  that that's it's not so straightforward  this example that I referred to earlier  is suggestive but it simply is pointing  out the fact that incentives are  different among different groups it's  not saying that if there was somehow  more equality in Indonesia there would  be you know more conservation that's a  hypothesis one might draw out of the  study but it's not a proof of that  anyways it's an interesting question to  think about coming back to to carbon  markets and I flagged them because they  are as I said one of the the biggest  most well integrated global  environmental markets created within the  last few years I think we can identify a  number of lessons that come out of that  for any kind of market creation for  ecosystem services one we've talked a  lot about is the issue of currency what  is it you're trading how do you measure  that secondly once you've defined what  it is you're going to trade what is the  ecosystem service that you're pricing  and transferring what are the approved  methodologies or recognized means of  delivering that  put that product if you're turning an  ecosystem service into a tradable  commodity you need some way for people  to say yes that is a legitimate way to  produce that commodity and in the carbon  market there are certain things you can  do that are recognized as delivering a  carbon credit and many other things that  people would like to do that they think  would generate a carbon credit but  they're not yet approved and you have to  go through a review process to get your  methodology approved as we discovered  with with the mangrove restoration  project with with Dan and that takes  quite some time costs quite a lot of  money to develop a methodology that  crosses all the t's and dot Saul the  eyes and gets the approval of the United  Nations and you also need alongside  those methodologies performance  standards to assess whether people are  actually following the methodology in  the approved manner and delivering as  one hopes the desired ecosystem service  and I think there's a lot of focus on  methodologies and performance standards  because very often we can't see or feel  or touch the ecosystem service so you  don't really know for sure how many tons  of carbon have been pulled out of the  atmosphere all you know is that somebody  has planted a bunch of trees and so you  have to say well did they plant the  approved species in the approved  planting pattern following the approved  methodologies if so we can by inference  assume that those carbon that carbon was  in fact locked up in those trees in the  quantities that they claim and used I  think the same is true for many  watershed protection schemes that we  can't measure directly the watershed  benefits sometimes you can measure  changes in water quality and quantity  but often that's expensive and you can't  do it on a regular basis so what you  measure instead is the change in land  use realistic targets and caps and  timetables is a critical issue here  we've seen the failure in the carbon  market of people to negotiate realistic  it's and timetables and that can upset  the market because it will discourage  investors from getting involved it  creates extra volatility in pricing and  that scares people off there's a variety  of instruments that can stimulate  private investment it's not just one in  the carbon market we've seen incentives  for energy efficiency we've seen  technology standards to try and promote  less energy intensive goods and services  we've seen tax credits we've seen the  trading scheme itself so it's it's not  always just one mechanism that is going  to deliver what we want but there may be  many related market mechanisms and  they're not always consistent with each  other monitoring enforcement is a key  issue you may have heard about a year  ago I think some people stole a bunch of  carbon credits from an account in Europe  and resold them in another country and  that was because there wasn't really a  very tight control on the accounting of  those carbon credits if you're going to  be trading and intangible good or  ecosystem service you have need to have  very tight control over the registries  that keep track of trades what's being  traded make sure it isn't traded or dug  again and again and particularly with  respect to social impacts we need to  have an adaptive approach because we  don't always anticipate the unintended  consequences on certain communities of  the actions we take and we need to  somehow make adjustments and you can see  this very clearly in the case of the  Mexican p.s scheme where when people see  how the system is set up they start to  try and manipulate it to their maximum  advantage and therefore the government  changes the system and adapts it  year-on-year to try and improve it to  get the outcomes that they want in terms  environmental terms and in social terms  you can't just  have one system and and assume that it's  going to continue working forever and in  the same way people will start playing  with it as soon as it's a alive  notwithstanding all those caveats and  challenges of payments for ecosystem  services we've seen meant more and more  countries set them up and these are  largely government funded schemes and  contrary to what some people have said  in in recent years that oh this is just  for rich countries in fact many  developing countries are putting quite  substantial sums of money into payments  for ecosystem service schemes including  in China something like four billion  dollars a year going into the sloping  land conversion program which is  essentially an incentive scheme to try  and reduce deforestation on steeply  sloped land reduce erosion runoff  siltation and sedimentation downstream  and at the other end of the scale you  have some very small programs in in  countries like Bulgaria and Romania in  Ecuador and I think it's important to  recognize that you don't need to have a  you know a huge program you don't need  to be a government with tremendous  capacity things can be done locally at a  relatively small scale that are  effective I talked last time about  biodiversity offsets and I just want her  to make a few comments on the evolution  of that and the emergence of these  trading schemes for habitat or for  ecosystems I'm aware that there are  people in the room who know more about  this than I do but a lot of people have  been looking recently outs from outside  the US at the experience in this country  with wetland mitigation banking it's  become a kind of test case or pilot  project a lot of European governments in  particular see it as as an example of  what they they could be doing themselves  and not necessarily exactly the same  so for those who don't know this is a  program that was authorized back in the  UU 70s under the Clean Water Act but  didn't really get going until the end of  the 1980s and didn't attract major  investment private investment till the  90s I believe but essentially the idea  is that if you impact a wetland as  determined by the federal government  what is a wetland in the United States  you have to compensate for that impact  and in general that compensation has to  be in kind that is to say you need to  restore conserve wetland equivalent  wetland typically more area than what  you have impacted and generally within  the same watershed or so what they call  service area as the the impact and  because you can do it yourself but you  can also contract a third party to do it  we've seen over the last 20 years or so  the emergence of a very lively trade in  wetland mitigation credits from  entrepreneurs mostly private  entrepreneurs who have acquired land  restored wetlands on that land obtained  credits from the government and turned  around and sold those to to developers  it's worth pointing out that a lot of  the purchasing has been by government  agencies particularly the transport  companies transport agencies at in the  states when they're building new roads  widening them for example they might  have an impact on wetlands and they need  to compensate for that under the same  legislation the value of this market is  really hard to tell because it's an  incredibly untransfected my sense is  that if you want to get information on  the price of particular trades the price  of credits it's not something you can  look up on Bloomberg right and maybe  that's an indication that it's still a  relatively immature market in that it's  not a it's not a very transparent market  nevertheless people have tried to gather  information  on this market and also on similar  initiatives in other countries so you  can see the little dots on the on the  map I can't remember what the  distinction is between the white dots  and the dark dots but essentially they  refer to different versions of  biodiversity offsets and have a chat  trading I think if I'm not mistaken the  the habitat trading ones are the darker  dots that is to say those are countries  in which governments have either set up  or are in the process of setting up  trading schemes rather than just one-off  offset schemes and as you can see  there's a concentration of experience in  North America Europe and Australia and  I'm looking forward to learning about  the Australian experience when I get  there very soon ok I've sort of reached  the end of the material that's in the  tube for business report and what I've  prepared for these lectures and I just  wanted to very quickly recap what we've  covered and some conclusions from that  if I may firstly we looked a little bit  at what's the business case why would  businesses be interested in biodiversity  and ecosystem services we've looked at  how companies and can understand the  magnitude of their impacts positive and  negative or their dependence on  ecosystem services we looked at a range  of tools that businesses have developed  on their own or in partnership with  others to try and avoid or mitigate  biodiversity risks and we've looked at  some of the emerging business  opportunities the the positive or good  news where there's an alignment between  business value and environmental value  we've looked at some of the target  setting and other voluntary actions that  businesses can take and I should  supplement that by pointing out that  more and more businesses are interested  in getting involved in discussions about  regulatory reform and there are  particularly interested in these  market-based approaches because they can  see how that might be aligned better  with their own investment strategies and  we've looked a little bit at some of the  different types  regulatory reforms that are in place or  being put in place and I just want to  conclude with a couple of thoughts on  aligning market incentives with  biodiversity conservation and the first  is I suppose the obvious that markets do  not deal very well with intangible or  non market value so you somehow need to  find a way to make the intangible the  aesthetic the spiritual the cultural if  you're willing to into something more  tangible and well we don't always know  what the boundaries are of what's  appropriate to bring into the market  what's appropriate to commodify if you  don't do that the market will continue  to ignore it for the most part and you  can try and build a fence around it but  then you have enforcement issues and we  can see that in many cases the the power  of the market overwhelms the capacity of  governments to enforce constraints on  resource use I think we've also seen  from experience that while voluntary  market initiatives can work relatively  well they're not always sufficient so  you can attract a certain level of  business commitment through  certification schemes through voluntary  carbon markets and what have you  voluntary biodiversity offsets but if  you really want to get scale and the I  think the Europeans proved this with the  emissions trading scheme if you want to  get significant scale of investment in  these ecosystem markets you need a  regulatory mandatory scheme you need  relatively powerful market incentives  not just wishful thinking and those  incentives they can be designed in such  a ways to attract private investment  they can be business friendly they can  be market oriented and they include  information instruments such as  certification and labeling and but also  reporting and disclosure requirements  they include fiscal policy reforms  undoing so-called perverse subsidies  such as subsidies for land conversion in  some countries or subsidies to the oil  and gas industry but you can also use  carrots  tax credits or other payments for  ecosystem services to try and encourage  people to do the right thing and a  particularly powerful mechanism for  creating incentives for the private  sector is reform of property rights and  in and liability and I include the two  together markets are predicated on  private property rights they're  predicated on the notion that you can  exclude people from certain resources  you control those and you can use those  in certain ways I'm not a lawyer I'm not  an expert in in in tenure and liability  by any means but I think it's clear that  if we're going to make progress on  environmental markets we need much more  collaboration with the law community on  the the design or redesign of resource  tenure and environmental liability  schemes and i also want to leave you  with some conclusions on the the poverty  angle because i think it's hard to have  a discussion about ecosystem markets  anywhere in the world today without also  getting into a discussion about poverty  development equity and distribution and  i think the thing they do go together  even though we don't always have the  analytical tools or the evidence base to  really understand what are those  interactions but I think we can draw  some conclusions from experience to date  and that is that if you want to have a  positive impact on the poorer  communities and and more vulnerable  societies you need to focus your your  business action on the places where they  live the sectors in which poorer groups  work such as agriculture forestry  fisheries the factors of production that  they own that's basically their own  labor to some extent land the outputs  that they can produce which again are  often commodities the markets that they  can participate in and the costs that  they can afford and I think similarly  when we look at what strategies are  going to achieve more Pro poor outcomes  in terms of interventions whether it's  by development agencies or  businesses first and foremost its  governance in the rule of law and  there's there's limits to what business  can do there but I think without that  rule of law we're not going to get  equitable outcomes let alone  environmentally effective outcomes  access to information and services  reducing constraints is often an  effective mechanism and we've seen this  in many developing countries where if  you want for example to get involved in  charcoal production or in sustainable  wood production or in sustainable  agriculture they're all kinds of  regulations that make it difficult for  people to to do that bigger companies  can afford to get through those  regulatory schemes poor producers  small-scale producers find it very  difficult so we've seen in in carbon  markets for example the development of  so-called small scale or yeah small  scale methodologies to try and get  around this this constraint on the  regulatory constraint on participation  of the poor participation is sort of a  mantra in the environmental movement we  always need to try and improve the  extent to which marginalized groups can  take part in decision making a big part  of that is looking at not just global  priorities but also local environmental  concerns which may be more to do with  clean water water supply access to  agricultural land rather than some of  these global concerns that we have about  biodiversity or climate and promoting  sustainable use rather than simply  strict conservation can be a useful  strategy as well that's a lot to try and  boil down into a couple of slides and  thank you for bearing with me from on  that I've reached the end of my  contribution to this course I want to  leave you with a list of the people who  should have been up here with me and  might have been even more eloquent than  I but these are the people who  contributed to the teep for Business  Report among the many other hundreds of  people who contributed to other cheap  reports and I think they also deserve a  of applause so thank you very much it's  been a privilege and a pleasure
 

